# UserPrompt Service Implementation Checklist

Status: Proposed implementation plan. The existing namespaced service-provider
framework is complete and is the required pattern for this service. This plan
does not reopen or redesign that framework.

Sources:

- Current DwarfUICore tooltip, context-menu, pointer, root-resolution,
  service-provider, reload, and package implementations
- `Docs/service-provider-api-proposal.md`, as documentation of the established
  provider conventions rather than unfinished work
- DwarfDirect targeting input behavior in sibling checkout `../DwarfDirect`
- DwarfUI mood-popover unit selection in sibling checkout `../DwarfUI`
- DFHack's native `df.global.game.main_interface.recenter_indicator_m`
  selection indicator

## Goal

Add a typed, namespace-bound `UserPromptServiceProvider` backed by one
process-wide `UserPrompt` service. Its first operation prompts the user to
select a location on the game map, displays a tooltip-like title and message
that follow below the pointer, and displays the game's native map-tile
selection indicator at the map position under the pointer.

The implementation must extend DwarfUICore's existing root-aware input and
completed-render mechanisms. It must not add a DwarfDirect-style fullscreen
overlay, a parallel hook chain, a normal tooltip registration, or a custom map
marker renderer.

## Locked behavior

- [ ] The public service and provider names are `UserPrompt` and
      `UserPromptServiceProvider`; do not use `UserActionPrompt`.
- [ ] The first operation is `prompt_map_location(options)`; do not introduce a
      generic action discriminator or string-dispatched prompt API.
- [ ] At most one prompt and one map-input consumer are active process-wide.
      The map-input consumer is active exactly while an accepted map-location
      prompt is pending.
- [ ] Prompt activation is atomic. If the required input or render transport
      cannot be established, reject the request and leave no pending prompt,
      input consumer, prompt window, or native indicator ownership behind.
- [ ] While active, `_MOUSE_L_DOWN` is always consumed and does nothing else.
      It does not sample, hit-test, complete, cancel, or delegate.
- [ ] While active, `_MOUSE_L` is always consumed. Its handler samples
      `dfhack.gui.getMousePos()` exactly once, copies the result, completes the
      prompt, and reports that result without focus, ownership, validity,
      gesture-latch, or non-nil conditions.
- [ ] A sampled position is reported as a detached `{x, y, z}` value or `nil`.
      A `nil` sample is a successful completion result, not cancellation.
- [ ] While active, `_MOUSE_R_DOWN` is always consumed and does not terminate
      the prompt. `_MOUSE_R` is always consumed and aborts the prompt exactly
      like Escape.
- [ ] While active, `LEAVESCREEN` is always consumed and aborts the prompt.
      This is the DFHack input boundary for pressing Escape.
- [ ] Cancellation by right-click, Escape, explicit API cancellation, or an
      approved lifecycle boundary invokes the cancellation callback at most
      once and never invokes the completion callback.
- [ ] Clear authoritative state, input ownership, prompt presentation, and
      native indicator ownership before invoking either terminal callback so a
      callback can synchronously open another prompt.
- [ ] Starting a prompt closes any open DwarfUICore context menu before the
      prompt becomes active. No context menu may open while the prompt is
      active.
- [ ] Prompt input has first refusal ahead of context-menu input. Consequently,
      prompt-owned right-click boundaries never reach context-menu opening or
      the base game.
- [ ] Inputs not owned by the active prompt delegate unchanged through the
      existing input chain. All input delegates unchanged when no prompt is
      active.
- [ ] A concurrent prompt request is rejected atomically; requests are never
      queued, replaced, or ordered by namespace.
- [ ] While active, the prompt window follows the screen pointer. Its preferred
      placement is directly below the pointer; viewport clamping may move it
      when the complete window cannot fit below or at an edge.
- [ ] While active and the pointer has a map position, directly assign that
      position to `recenter_indicator_m`. Do not call
      `dfhack.gui.revealInDwarfmodeMap()`, pan the map, change z-level, or alter
      follow state.
- [ ] When no map position is under the pointer, hide the prompt-owned native
      indicator using the verified native inactive representation. The prompt
      remains active.
- [ ] Snapshot pre-existing native indicator state before the prompt's first
      write. Before every later write, verify the current value still equals
      the prompt's last write. If another system changed it, relinquish
      indicator ownership for the rest of the prompt. On termination, restore
      the snapshot only if the prompt still owns the current value.
- [ ] The click completion handler always takes a fresh map sample. It never
      substitutes the position cached for the visual indicator.
- [ ] Tooltip and context-menu behavior remains unchanged while no prompt is
      active.

## Project constraints

- [ ] Reuse the completed service-provider acquisition, immutable API,
      namespace, identity, generation, error, and reload patterns. Do not
      redesign or reimplement them.
- [ ] Before generalizing any shared input or render type, show from the current
      implementation that the exposed seam cannot safely host both existing
      behavior and UserPrompt. Keep the change to the smallest sufficient
      internal abstraction.
- [ ] Preserve the process-wide tooltip and context-menu backends and all
      unrelated worktree/index state. Do not stash or commit.
- [ ] Production modules start with `--@ module=true`, use `reqscript()` for
      project modules, and are registered in dependency order.
- [ ] Every new or modified class and method has LuaDoc. Every closed
      discriminator set is an immutable numeric enum.
- [ ] Public objects, request snapshots, handles, and callback-visible values
      follow existing immutability, namespace, and runtime-generation rules.
- [ ] Public APIs expose no private hooks, renderers, indicator owners,
      diagnostics, or callbacks belonging to other namespaces.
- [ ] Unit, syntax, build, package, live automation, rendered-screen evidence,
      installed-runtime evidence, and cleanup remain distinct gates.
- [ ] Perform the required follow-up review after each implementation section
      before marking that section complete.

## Out of scope

- [ ] Do not add unit, item, building, area, list-choice, confirmation, text,
      quantity, or multi-location prompts.
- [ ] Do not add queues, public priorities, implicit replacement, timeouts,
      persistence, coroutine suspension, promises, or blocking calls.
- [ ] Do not add consumer-supplied position validation or rejection messages.
- [ ] Do not migrate or modify DwarfDirect, DwarfUI, or another consumer.
- [ ] Do not expose prompt state through the tooltip or context-menu APIs.
- [ ] Do not use `renderMapOverlay()`, a custom tile glyph, or
      `revealInDwarfmodeMap()` for the active location indicator.

## Phase 0 - Record the implementation contract and baseline

- [ ] Record branch, revision, worktree/index state, package version, registry
      order, and installed-runtime resolution before production edits.
- [ ] Record the current complete unit, syntax, offline build, package, focused
      tooltip live, and focused context-menu live baselines.
- [ ] Document the exact version 1 UserPrompt API schema, following the
      established provider pattern: constructor contract, namespace binding,
      `get_namespace()`, `prompt_map_location(options)`, `cancel(handle)`,
      `is_active(handle)`, and `clear_namespace()`.
- [ ] Define title, message, completion callback, cancellation callback, option
      copying, unknown-field rejection, and empty/size constraints without
      altering the completed cross-service provider rules.
- [ ] Define the stable busy error, handle validation precedence, and the
      return behavior of `cancel`, `is_active`, and `clear_namespace` for
      active, terminal, foreign, malformed, and stale handles.
- [ ] Record every lifecycle boundary and whether it invokes cancellation:
      explicit cancel, namespace cleanup, world unload, input-root loss, and
      Core reload. No boundary may leave an invisible active consumer.
- [ ] Define composite key precedence as: terminal cancellation
      (`LEAVESCREEN` or `_MOUSE_R`) before `_MOUSE_L` completion, then consumed
      nonterminal down boundaries. Test combinations even if DFHack normally
      emits them separately.
- [ ] Verify the target DFHack version's active/inactive representation and
      rendering semantics for `recenter_indicator_m` before encoding them.
- [ ] Map the exact production, test, registry, reload, documentation, and
      package files expected to change.

### Phase 0 completion gate

- [ ] The UserPrompt-specific public schema and every locked behavior are
      explicit, while the completed provider framework remains unchanged.
- [ ] Baseline failures and unrelated worktree changes are recorded rather
      than attributed to UserPrompt.
- [ ] The follow-up review finds no conditional left-click completion, pending
      but inert state, context-menu coexistence, or ambiguous right-click path.

## Phase 1 - Characterize and select the smallest shared seams

- [ ] Protect the current context-menu pre-delegation semantics with tests:
      native and Lua-screen transport selection, exact predecessor arguments
      and return values, root reconciliation, foreign wrappers, reload
      adoption, and failure containment.
- [ ] Protect the current tooltip completed-render semantics with tests:
      native, overlay, and Lua-screen selection; completed draw order; motion;
      resize; clamping; invalidation; and reload adoption.
- [ ] Characterize current context-menu close/open APIs so prompt activation can
      synchronously close an existing menu and active prompt input can suppress
      every input-driven opening path.
- [ ] Characterize current pointer and map sampling, including a nil
      `dfhack.gui.getMousePos()` result.
- [ ] Record why the context-menu hook's current single-handler contract is or
      is not sufficient for prompt-first arbitration before editing it.
- [ ] Record why the tooltip hook's current single-presenter contract is or is
      not sufficient for independent prompt presentation before editing it.
- [ ] Preserve only the applicable DwarfDirect lesson: a pre-delegation handler
      consumes by returning `true`. Do not copy its fullscreen overlay,
      targeting validation, destination rejection, or marker renderer.
- [ ] Protect the DwarfUI/DFHack native-indicator behavior with a focused seam
      test or injected indicator adapter before integration.

### Phase 1 completion gate

- [ ] The selected input/render changes are justified by concrete insufficiency
      in current seams and are no broader than needed.
- [ ] Characterization tests detect changes to predecessor delegation,
      transport selection, render order, context-menu exclusion, and indicator
      ownership behavior.
- [ ] The follow-up review confirms all existing behavior needed by tooltip and
      context-menu remains protected.

## Phase 2 - Add prompt-first input arbitration

- [ ] Generalize or adapt the existing context-menu input-hook owner into one
      reload-safe internal dispatcher only if Phase 1 proves it necessary; do
      not create another native or Lua-screen trampoline chain.
- [ ] Give the active UserPrompt consumer deterministic first refusal and the
      context-menu opener deterministic later refusal. Keep this priority
      private.
- [ ] Stop dispatch at the first `true`; invoke the predecessor exactly once
      with all original arguments and return values only when every consumer
      returns false.
- [ ] Keep inactive prompt dispatch transparent and preserve current
      context-menu behavior when no prompt is active.
- [ ] Ensure prompt-owned input remains consumed if its handler throws. Record
      the failure, terminate the prompt safely, and return `true` so the event
      cannot leak to context-menu or base-game handling.
- [ ] Preserve existing fail-open behavior for failures from consumers that do
      not currently own the event, unless characterization proves a different
      established contract.
- [ ] Reconcile hooks with the current map-capable input surface before
      publishing active prompt state. Complete every fallible preparation
      before the activation commit and roll back cleanly on setup failure.
- [ ] Update process ownership, retirement, module registry, package contract,
      and diagnostics without leaving the old context-menu hook live in
      parallel.

### Phase 2 completion gate

- [ ] Tests prove one active native trampoline, no duplicate dispatch, exact
      predecessor behavior, prompt-first ordering, and inactive transparency
      across construction and Core reload.
- [ ] Existing context-menu input unit and live tests pass through the selected
      shared seam.
- [ ] The follow-up review verifies that a prompt-owned input cannot reach a
      later consumer or predecessor even on internal failure.

## Phase 3 - Add independent prompt presentation

- [ ] Extend the existing completed-render owner with the smallest internal
      multi-presenter/compositor seam proven necessary in Phase 1; do not add a
      second render-hook chain or route the prompt through tooltip registration.
- [ ] Preserve current tooltip selection and rendering exactly when no prompt
      is active.
- [ ] Define deterministic draw order for simultaneous ordinary tooltip and
      prompt intents. The prompt window must remain visible and must not corrupt
      or overwrite tooltip renderer state.
- [ ] Add an input-transparent prompt renderer with separate title and message,
      wrapping, frame/background styling, preferred below-pointer placement,
      and viewport clamping.
- [ ] Recompute layout for relevant text, pointer, and viewport changes and
      invalidate promptly on activation, motion, and termination.
- [ ] Preserve one completed-render call per selected owner and reload-safe
      adoption for native, overlay, and Lua-screen roots.
- [ ] Update teardown, registry, package contract, and diagnostics for the
      selected shared owner.

### Phase 3 completion gate

- [ ] Existing tooltip unit and final-render tests pass unchanged.
- [ ] Prompt render tests cover distinct title/message text, preferred below
      placement, all-edge clamping, pointer motion, resizing, exact draw order,
      and input transparency on every supported surface.
- [ ] The follow-up review confirms there is one render-hook owner, no tooltip
      registration for the prompt, and no ambiguous renderer arbitration.

## Phase 4 - Implement UserPrompt state and native indicator ownership

- [ ] Add one process-wide state machine with immutable numeric internal enums
      for closed prompt-state and terminal-cause sets.
- [ ] Copy and freeze each accepted request and allocate an opaque handle using
      existing service kind, contract-major, namespace, local identity, and
      runtime-generation conventions.
- [ ] Reject a concurrent request before changing input, presentation, context
      menu, or indicator state.
- [ ] Implement one idempotent terminal transition for completion,
      cancellation, namespace cleanup, root loss, world unload, failure, and
      reload retirement.
- [ ] Make terminal state observable before callbacks and ensure exact-once,
      mutually exclusive callback dispatch with callback failure containment.
- [ ] Add an injected native-indicator adapter that snapshots a detached prior
      value before its first prompt write and records the last value written by
      the prompt.
- [ ] Before each later indicator write, compare the native value with the
      prompt's last write. On mismatch, record external takeover, stop writing,
      and do not restore the prompt's snapshot at termination.
- [ ] Update `recenter_indicator_m` directly from the current map position and
      use the verified inactive value when the pointer is not over the map.
- [ ] On every terminal path, restore the snapshot only when the current native
      value still equals the prompt's last write. Otherwise relinquish
      ownership without changing the external value.
- [ ] Do not use the native indicator as authoritative prompt state or as the
      completion result.
- [ ] Add private diagnostics for state, identity, selected transports,
      terminal cause/counts, input counts, render counts, callback failures,
      and indicator ownership without exposing callback data.

### Phase 4 completion gate

- [ ] Tests cover atomic acceptance, busy rejection, each terminal cause,
      reentrant callbacks, callback failures, and exact cleanup order.
- [ ] Indicator tests cover prior-state snapshot, hover updates, nil-pointer
      hiding, owned restoration, external takeover, and all terminal paths.
- [ ] The follow-up review confirms no path produces both callbacks, leaves an
      active consumer, or overwrites an indicator no longer owned by the
      prompt.

## Phase 5 - Add the provider, facade, and runtime integration

- [ ] Add `USER_PROMPT` to the existing private immutable service-kind enum and
      extend existing acquisition, runtime cache, identity, error, and reload
      mechanisms without changing their contracts for current services.
- [ ] Add the version 1 private adapter and immutable
      `UserPromptServiceProvider` using the exact construction structure of the
      tooltip and context-menu providers.
- [ ] Export `UserPromptServiceProvider` from
      `reqscript('dwarfuicore/services')` alongside the existing providers;
      keep `reqscript('dwarfuicore')` free of service APIs.
- [ ] Implement the locked namespace-bound API and validate/copy all public
      arguments before lazy service initialization or state mutation.
- [ ] Reject malformed, stale, and foreign handles with established error
      precedence and stable category tokens.
- [ ] Ensure repeated provider construction reuses one healthy UserPrompt
      backend without replacing hooks, presenters, existing services, or
      current state.
- [ ] Add modules in dependency order and update provider, runtime, reload,
      registry, documentation, and package tests.

### Phase 5 completion gate

- [ ] Provider tests prove distinct immutable namespace-bound APIs, lazy
      transactional acquisition, generation safety, and singleton reuse.
- [ ] Existing tooltip and context-menu APIs gain no methods and retain their
      namespace state and runtime owners.
- [ ] The follow-up review compares the implementation with both the locked
      UserPrompt schema and the current provider implementation pattern.

## Phase 6 - Implement exact input and context-menu exclusion

- [ ] Activate the prompt input consumer only after its input surface is ready;
      keep it registered and authoritative for the entire pending interval.
- [ ] `_MOUSE_L_DOWN`: return `true` immediately with no sampling, terminal
      transition, callback, later consumer, or predecessor call.
- [ ] `_MOUSE_L`: sample once in the handler, copy `{x, y, z}` or nil, clear all
      prompt ownership, return `true`, and dispatch completion.
- [ ] Accept `_MOUSE_L` without a preceding down event and do not latch or
      compare press/release positions.
- [ ] `_MOUSE_R_DOWN`: return `true` immediately without terminating.
- [ ] `_MOUSE_R` and `LEAVESCREEN`: clear all prompt ownership, return `true`,
      and dispatch cancellation without sampling a completion position.
- [ ] Apply the locked composite-key precedence and exact-once terminal rules.
- [ ] Do not consult hover ownership, blockers, focus allowlists, map validity,
      tile flags, loaded blocks, reachability, or consumer validation before
      consuming or completing.
- [ ] Close an already-open context menu as part of successful prompt
      activation after fallible transport preparation and immediately before
      publishing active state. No failing operation may remain between menu
      closure and publication of the committed prompt state.
- [ ] Prove neither context-menu opening nor base-game handling receives left,
      right, or Escape boundaries owned by an active prompt.
- [ ] Delegate wheel, movement, pause, and every other unowned input unchanged.

### Phase 6 completion gate

- [ ] Focused tests prove unconditional left-down consumption, unconditional
      left completion including nil, right-down consumption, right-release
      cancellation, Escape cancellation, and composite-key precedence.
- [ ] Tests prove an existing context menu closes on activation and no context
      menu can open until the prompt terminates.
- [ ] The follow-up review searches for forbidden validation, latching,
      conditional consumption, cached-position completion, and owned-event
      delegation.

## Phase 7 - Integrate pointer-following UI, indicator, and lifecycle

- [ ] Publish prompt render intent and take native-indicator ownership only as
      part of successful activation; invalidate immediately.
- [ ] Drive prompt placement from the current screen pointer and the native
      indicator from the current map pointer while pending.
- [ ] Keep visual map sampling independent from the fresh completion sample and
      make a nil visual sample hide only the indicator.
- [ ] Render the requested title/message with preferred below-pointer placement
      and deterministic clamping without claiming below placement at an edge
      where the complete window cannot fit.
- [ ] Clear input ownership, render intent, and indicator ownership before
      callbacks on mouse completion, right-click/Escape cancellation, explicit
      cancel, namespace cleanup, root loss, world unload, failure, and reload.
- [ ] Do not transfer an active prompt to an unrelated UI root. If its required
      input/render surface disappears, apply the documented lifecycle
      cancellation policy immediately.
- [ ] Retire UserPrompt state before retiring shared input/render owners during
      explicit Core reload.
- [ ] Clean partial initialization without deleting foreign wrappers,
      presentations, registrations, context-menu state, or indicator values.

### Phase 7 completion gate

- [ ] Integration tests prove input, window, and indicator activate and retire
      together on every terminal path and through callback reentry.
- [ ] Render tests prove the prompt is visible and pointer-following on each
      supported surface and absent after completion or cancellation.
- [ ] The follow-up review verifies lifecycle order, generation checks, root
      ownership, context-menu exclusion, and ownership-aware indicator restore.

## Phase 8 - Documentation, package, and automated qualification

- [ ] Document provider acquisition, exact option/callback schema, nil
      completion, input consumption, right-click/Escape cancellation,
      context-menu exclusion, explicit cancellation, busy behavior, native
      indicator behavior, lifecycle cleanup, callback safety, and reload.
- [ ] Update README examples, service-provider documentation only where needed
      to list the new established-pattern provider, module registry, package
      inventory, and package version without reviving deleted planning docs.
- [ ] Add focused unit suites for provider/facade, state, input arbitration,
      prompt rendering, context-menu exclusion, indicator ownership, failure,
      lifecycle, and reload.
- [ ] Add focused DwarfSpec scenarios for visible prompt rendering and native
      indicator movement; left-down suppression; left completion including the
      nil sampling seam; right-click/Escape abort; context-menu exclusion;
      callback reentry; lifecycle cleanup; and reload.
- [ ] Run focused tooltip and context-menu unit/live regressions after the
      internal input/render changes.
- [ ] Run complete unit and DwarfSpec suites with terminal structured results
      and `cleanup_confirmed` recorded separately.
- [ ] Run Lua syntax, offline build, expanded package verification, archive
      verification, installed resolution checks, and `git diff --check`.
- [ ] Inspect the built and installed package for every UserPrompt/shared module
      and confirm no obsolete parallel hook owner is present.

### Phase 8 completion gate

- [ ] Focused and complete results are recorded separately; no unit/model test
      is reported as live rendered or installed-runtime success.
- [ ] Package contents, documentation, LuaDoc, registry/reload order, and the
      locked contract agree.
- [ ] The follow-up review finds no stale name, debug code, unauthorized
      consumer migration, deleted-doc reference, or unrecorded artifact.

## Phase 9 - Live acceptance and independent final review

- [ ] Install the exact verified package and use a fresh DFHack process when
      installed resolution changes.
- [ ] Capture final-screen evidence that distinctive title/message content
      follows below the pointer where space permits and clamps at every edge.
- [ ] Capture the native tile indicator moving with map hover, hiding off-map,
      and restoring its prior value after each terminal path without panning,
      z-level change, or follow-state change.
- [ ] Prove `_MOUSE_L_DOWN` cannot reach base-game behavior and leaves the
      prompt pending.
- [ ] Prove `_MOUSE_L` consumes, reports the fresh sampler result including nil,
      removes prompt visuals, and does not trigger base-game click behavior.
- [ ] Prove right-click and Escape each consume, cancel exactly once, remove
      prompt visuals, and leave no active input consumer.
- [ ] Prove an existing context menu closes on activation and right-click cannot
      open another while the prompt is active; verify context-menu behavior
      returns after termination.
- [ ] Prove representative unowned input delegates unchanged.
- [ ] Exercise completion and cancellation callbacks that synchronously start a
      replacement prompt and verify the replacement is active and visible.
- [ ] Exercise world unload, root loss, namespace cleanup, and explicit Core
      reload; inspect service, input, render, menu, indicator, hook, and screen
      state after each.
- [ ] Perform a second independent review against every locked behavior,
      completion gate, diff, and evidence row.
- [ ] Remove temporary fixtures, installed test state, active prompts, screens,
      hooks, disposable registrations, and logs requiring cleanup; record
      `cleanup_confirmed`.

### Phase 9 completion gate

- [ ] Final rendered-screen evidence proves the prompt window and native map
      indicator behavior; layout or adapter unit tests alone do not.
- [ ] Installed-runtime evidence proves exact input consumption, completion,
      both cancellation gestures, context-menu exclusion, callback reentry,
      reload safety, restoration, and cleanup.
- [ ] The independent review reports no unmet checklist item or unexplained
      contract divergence.

## Definition of done

- [ ] `reqscript('dwarfuicore/services')` exports the existing providers plus
      immutable `UserPromptServiceProvider` through the established pattern.
- [ ] `UserPromptServiceProvider:new(1, namespace)` returns a distinct immutable
      namespace-bound API backed by one process-wide UserPrompt runtime.
- [ ] `prompt_map_location()` accepts exactly the documented title, message,
      completion, and cancellation contract and returns an opaque owned handle.
- [ ] Only one prompt is pending process-wide; activation is atomic and cannot
      leave a pending-but-inert prompt.
- [ ] `_MOUSE_L_DOWN` always consumes without sampling or completing.
- [ ] `_MOUSE_L` always consumes and completes with one fresh copied
      `{x, y, z}|nil` sample without conditions.
- [ ] `_MOUSE_R_DOWN` always consumes without terminating; `_MOUSE_R` and
      Escape always consume and cancel.
- [ ] An open context menu closes when a prompt starts, no context menu opens
      while it is active, and normal context-menu behavior resumes afterward.
- [ ] Unowned input delegates unchanged and all prompt-owned input remains
      consumed even if prompt dispatch fails.
- [ ] The title/message window follows below the pointer where space permits,
      clamps within the viewport, and remains input-transparent.
- [ ] The prompt directly drives `recenter_indicator_m` from current map hover,
      hides it off-map, never calls `revealInDwarfmodeMap()`, and restores prior
      state only while retaining ownership.
- [ ] Completion always uses a fresh sample rather than the visual indicator's
      cached position.
- [ ] State, input, presentation, and indicator ownership clear before exact-
      once mutually exclusive terminal callback dispatch.
- [ ] Handles enforce namespace, service, contract-major, and generation
      ownership; public APIs expose no private infrastructure.
- [ ] Namespace cleanup, root loss, world unload, failure, and reload leave no
      hidden prompt, active consumer, stale presentation, indicator damage, or
      duplicate hook.
- [ ] Existing tooltip and context-menu unit/live/render/reload/package
      contracts remain green through the smallest justified shared changes.
- [ ] Every new or modified class/method has LuaDoc, all closed discriminators
      are immutable numeric enums, and all modules follow loading/registry
      conventions.
- [ ] Focused unit, complete unit, syntax, offline build, expanded package,
      archive, focused live, complete live, rendered-screen, installed-runtime,
      reload, cleanup, and diff evidence pass as distinct gates.
- [ ] Required follow-up reviews and the final independent review confirm the
      implementation before this checklist is marked complete.

